#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166634 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "e8353dba-48d7-5f4f-8ba5-97e4e22b6e44" :question/math-academy-id "e-9050" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\, \\rm{ m/s}$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\sin^2 x \\,\\rm{m/s}^2.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ what differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/f187eff194f136838b67ab12b44f8f6882eafb59885a5c3f440d41492def4a42.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\sin^2 x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = -\\sin^2 x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166635 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "49e02115-6211-564b-8221-d5835a98a064" :question/math-academy-id "e-9051" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x \\,\\rm{m},$ its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s}$ and its acceleration is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2.$ If $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m,$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x = 1 \\, \\textrm m.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/4fdd70a4ca80965c0adce9c60be36b0712852b662cff69f9733f50c739fd1b32.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 2x + e^{x-2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2x + e^{x-2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+{e}^{x-2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2 = x^2 + e^{x-2} + C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={2}^{2}+{e}^{2-2}+C \\\\ 8 & =4+1+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x) \\gt 0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{2\\left(x^2 + e^{x-2} + 3\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x = 1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({1}^{2}+{e}^{1-2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(4+{e}^{-1})} \\\\  & ≈2.96\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166644 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/id #uuid "80b653d0-5dff-5621-86ee-e1fd336ad740" :question/math-academy-id "e-9455" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\geq 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\textrm m,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/eca36626e6a0cfb30d293e684483c37ddce6f18d4004c3f77258e9663565d8d7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =-\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = -\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when $x=0\\, \\textrm{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =\\frac{2}{0+1}+K \\\\ 2 & =2+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{x+1} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x) > 0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}} \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2 \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1)^{3/2} & =2t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = \\dfrac{3C_1}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/2} =3t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+1 \\\\ x+1 & =(3t+1)^{2/3} \\\\ x & =(3t+1)^{2/3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6b8daa4-a421-5ee6-82b9-9eb043ad7cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = 2\\sqrt{x^{2} + 12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163419} {:answer/id #uuid "ca01366f-9c8a-51ce-8f15-bed65200a620" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = 2\\sqrt{x^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163420} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe873e5-d7ad-55ae-91e9-d36486402d46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = 2x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163421} {:answer/id #uuid "13d41fac-776e-5c91-a0ac-84fdd7cd9133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = \\frac{8}{5}\\sqrt{x^{2} + 21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163422} {:answer/id #uuid "20b8760e-1b1a-5405-bed3-21cdbc66936b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = x^{2} + 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6b8daa4-a421-5ee6-82b9-9eb043ad7cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x) = 2\\sqrt{x^{2} + 12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163419} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eed9cef-29c7-5b9e-b2e1-4fece31a3301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfc6326b-3536-5d27-a880-335a706849db" :question/math-academy-id "q-107999" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $2\\,m$ when its velocity is $8\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/017463f3ac8abac9c2b00c4d9a715a4109a5f4bc1b070e0a12c4d3a7bac64351.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=2\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=8\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =2(2)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =32-8 \\\\ C & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+24 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{x}^{2}+48 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({x}^{2}+12) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+12}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4448af6-7baf-5a5b-b8d1-39a077ebf8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.1m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163425} {:answer/id #uuid "c9857de1-4db2-5ad9-a25d-dde1b667afaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.7m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163426} {:answer/id #uuid "f1bea300-34aa-57f5-afe8-1a6e6ecf1c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.2m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163427} {:answer/id #uuid "2f61cdd9-b058-5af6-b23f-8b8773806d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163428} {:answer/id #uuid "4113fec4-4e93-5ab7-8a45-bc6f10d75a97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.4m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9857de1-4db2-5ad9-a25d-dde1b667afaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.7m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163426} :answer-field/id #uuid "086d497a-6cd2-50e5-a303-5b7f3ca50234" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c92f4b3-2f0f-5819-981c-8fa3d450f8a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-108002" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2+3{e}^{-x})\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=2\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=3\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/ec4af1357e75dcee6d7d5412467c2ee2ea9e54fa38fb6e83817b0ae909c529aa.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2+3{e}^{-x})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2+3{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2+3{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2+3{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2x-3{e}^{-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =2(0)-3{e}^{-0}+C \\\\ 2 & =-3+C \\\\ C & =2+3 \\\\ C & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2x-3{e}^{-x}+5 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4x-6{e}^{-x}+10 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4x-6{e}^{-x}+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4x-6{e}^{-x}+10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4(3)-6{e}^{-3}+10} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{22-6{e}^{-3}} \\\\ v & ≈4.7\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "832f5079-56a5-5633-a36a-abcaa350c6f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2}) = 6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163437} {:answer/id #uuid "ece83403-1738-5762-a14b-2869fedf272b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dx} = 6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163438} {:answer/id #uuid "032ae97f-c542-562e-bb45-fad43b5fc7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(v^{2}) =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163439} {:answer/id #uuid "342bdb71-912c-58bd-a61f-2bf43bf15640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dx} =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163440} {:answer/id #uuid "366f26c4-1e76-5037-be3b-fe4e333ae3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2}) =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "366f26c4-1e76-5037-be3b-fe4e333ae3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2}) =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163441} :answer-field/id #uuid "5043cace-708e-584e-9f93-2239e526e2c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cde0f0e6-e59d-5767-8e42-f1f093f582b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-108010" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $6{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/52fc197612dac35373ecebf771b8444505902b5c6e00c7c55d2b21dab4809136.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $6{x}^{3}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-6{x}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2a0c9ee-1435-58ad-9db8-1f41c5a1608a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314055} {:answer/id #uuid "6336ee57-0862-5ed8-87c5-d97d62829324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314056} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a83a2d-1a74-51a5-9b5e-2e254e317731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314057} {:answer/id #uuid "88bee9e1-399b-58ee-8989-94bbd93457c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314058} {:answer/id #uuid "05b1ee62-939c-5834-bf2f-140c3581deb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6336ee57-0862-5ed8-87c5-d97d62829324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314056} :answer-field/id #uuid "23013f89-b89e-5b9a-a50d-5890430c37c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "985c21fe-e314-5497-89ec-0da4b5f6b75a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122305" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,\\text{m/s}$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{-2x}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/dd924518ae28ee8bf010e76b95ddbd68a26137b8b1f28753900455a87e1df799.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{-2x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0967453-aa49-5924-8e36-ab4317ea6b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314062} {:answer/id #uuid "84f5cccb-dc85-552b-8c0e-b6e7e907b4c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314063} {:answer/id #uuid "83a3e83d-eeb3-5998-a7cc-2e6b2c5041a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314064} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9e1f87-83cd-5482-bea8-3f859ac4bdef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314065} {:answer/id #uuid "56fa7dfb-62ab-5c14-9dd8-28eef246c493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0967453-aa49-5924-8e36-ab4317ea6b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314062} :answer-field/id #uuid "689f7648-9ab6-56be-957c-aa1103a4b570" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04e631b6-54cf-5621-89ae-3917dfc5dc9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122310" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${cos}^{2}⁡x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/768db6b8051d8f4bea528f6c5b5d386ffc0d6529517d2fe72641cb33f6e8ac10.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ceb8256-c17a-53e1-ac22-32f6f795df58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314069} {:answer/id #uuid "1d18169b-8026-562c-a919-850efef7b461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314070} {:answer/id #uuid "b177d108-a207-50df-9c89-8b321579c5ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314071} {:answer/id #uuid "f9552b99-b391-5ece-9344-829c730e0c50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314072} {:answer/id #uuid "01416141-3538-5a15-97ee-0eb2e61d5c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi +t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d18169b-8026-562c-a919-850efef7b461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314070} :answer-field/id #uuid "01399d4a-955d-5d24-b875-b8a046ac00c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1494f900-5c6d-561b-9ba9-7d9115f8d044" :question/math-academy-id "q-122312" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+4{x}^{2})\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t$ where $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{8}].$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/59c4a51d419b6e42633579b687cde16a2842895c589f818942c10aa304bc98a7.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+4{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2x & =t+K \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=0,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30dda7a5-4016-5e40-9224-3c6279cdd41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314076} {:answer/id #uuid "db3b5ecf-1216-57f7-94c9-70c9b7c34f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314077} {:answer/id #uuid "7c730bf0-872c-5722-bdec-13ef6599263d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314078} {:answer/id #uuid "2096a1f6-beb6-516f-98b7-c133fda87158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314079} {:answer/id #uuid "762a12c9-05cb-5020-8b40-db0ca1b94098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2096a1f6-beb6-516f-98b7-c133fda87158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314079} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ca2bad5-eebc-557a-a58f-638f93fcb85c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05e75a3d-3e2e-5128-ae1f-6d92d30f38f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122313" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/00da8cdff7814df7368ff00924360db24a93934c5ae1c87ceb5daa6b00df37a7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0f9e10f-81f8-5eb5-a68a-d90a070f19b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314083} {:answer/id #uuid "9fdc8b3d-cc9f-5b98-ab40-99bb47c0eb58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314084} {:answer/id #uuid "02ac20cd-bd74-5742-8b19-0ec599a24f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314085} {:answer/id #uuid "d1def38b-cfcd-58df-8efc-8af24f5e934a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314086} {:answer/id #uuid "24cf1ab2-5761-5250-bf0d-c8c08c4244a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fdc8b3d-cc9f-5b98-ab40-99bb47c0eb58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314084} :answer-field/id #uuid "4759492e-6d28-5a9e-b0c3-df20068a67e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fca9354-5321-5542-b5b1-39625f1faa01" :question/math-academy-id "q-122315" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(3{e}^{x}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/785776cee4f82c5bfa5e86b5b1cb746b7c80f2d702fb52f67055a60bdff249ae.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(3{e}^{x}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{x}+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{x}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5acf374f-291a-5639-a46b-7acac2eef5a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314090} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f8ec42-40f1-55df-a2d2-6d0cfd24fbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314091} {:answer/id #uuid "01be0c81-03f1-5b16-afe3-01d09fe19fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314092} {:answer/id #uuid "3c860876-8077-5d8a-b490-1124115ea5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314093} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc2e683-94fa-5538-ac1d-59eb33666c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8f8ec42-40f1-55df-a2d2-6d0cfd24fbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314091} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1a84703-3c19-5a54-b80b-5eeb8b58eb55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f818b71-1c62-567d-b5ef-4300712765ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122325" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)={e}^{-2x}\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/8faba5a783797f7025f51ec1ae587cb68355dff7138278689346c2f713275562.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{-2x}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x} & =t+K \\\\ {e}^{2x} & =2t+C \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56fe543e-e026-5266-a271-24932d9b1e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314097} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4a2c70-469e-5ffc-87f6-84c5cbbc4d59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{3{e}^{x-3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314098} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3928b8-a77e-5319-b69a-404239609725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=3{e}^{x-3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314099} {:answer/id #uuid "d7cf0967-6ed2-591f-b85d-a076aa8110f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={e}^{x-3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314100} {:answer/id #uuid "b253ed67-676c-5100-b57b-d40fec708aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{{e}^{x-3}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56fe543e-e026-5266-a271-24932d9b1e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314097} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea53bf7f-7eb2-545c-a38e-16d9d8e43c05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b97a4f6-ee90-5fc9-94b5-f52f95caa841" :question/math-academy-id "q-122337" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $3\\,m$ when its velocity is $2\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/164c90893a07ae9657e90b6f2b25e53c9c4927b1f4a076429ba0521a98377f24.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={e}^{x-3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=3\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & ={e}^{3-3}+C \\\\ 2 & =1+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+1 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({e}^{x-3}+1) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3221189b-a6e2-50f8-962a-f1b66bf2d35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314104} {:answer/id #uuid "1efc99f4-8d88-5a1b-aa8b-b022f299f233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314105} {:answer/id #uuid "d32b0d5c-2a5e-5401-b79a-3413e6f805dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314106} {:answer/id #uuid "1d85b192-c773-51bd-9307-3bf0ed37eebc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314107} {:answer/id #uuid "547208ea-728c-58ba-98e1-159e175ba763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d32b0d5c-2a5e-5401-b79a-3413e6f805dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314106} :answer-field/id #uuid "73602914-89c6-539e-8096-7272b5eeb3b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4236c426-f250-51ed-a60b-d78d2f8899ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-122343" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/0dd98b517e2ed800f12a3672d2d6f45021f7f502cfe98c1e9308e52c05ab5f38.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{e}^{x/2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since  $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =2{e}^{0/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =2+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{e}^{x/2}+1 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4{e}^{1/2}+1} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{4\\sqrt{e}+1} \\\\  & ≈2.8\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7992ead3-e5f7-547e-9efc-270d787d50bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{t}{1-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314111} {:answer/id #uuid "29133973-97fb-5199-9500-d43e5e83d14b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2t-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314112} {:answer/id #uuid "8efdf446-ae54-51a7-a71e-2615d299ff1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{4t}{1-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314113} {:answer/id #uuid "8a278244-4267-5d36-9aca-7ad4a24407b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314114} {:answer/id #uuid "46993d2a-bae4-5320-96e0-15424b25d131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=t-4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8efdf446-ae54-51a7-a71e-2615d299ff1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{4t}{1-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314113} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda73ff6-3a44-5506-bf98-66be2ece3c2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e97459ef-d10a-5b95-843c-7ee0d441d495" :question/math-academy-id "q-122344" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $2(x+2)^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t$ where $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{4}].$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/ba9988a12d1c70c43a6a010c3578850fe79a9bca01309884fe981b3f7cca512c.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2(x+2)^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2(x+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2(x+2)^{3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2(x+2)^{3}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+2)^{4}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+2)^{4} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =(x+2)^{4} \\\\ v & =\\pm (x+2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=(x+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(x+2)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x+2} & =t+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t-C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+2}=-t+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+2} & =-t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{1-2t}{2} \\\\ x+2 & =\\frac{2}{1-2t} \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-2t}-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4t}{1-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acfac3bb-0043-5474-baec-f7d9aad9505b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2(t+2)^{3}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314118} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3a5543-4bab-5985-ac81-4fe61f28822a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{t+8}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314119} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf1bff2-b361-5c1b-9cf1-6ff1b6485139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+1)^{3}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314120} {:answer/id #uuid "83791ddf-ad3a-5a14-a851-64280f33ea6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=9-\\sqrt[3]{t+8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314121} {:answer/id #uuid "96855702-d802-5259-a1ca-7f4b6d0a3c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+2)^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96855702-d802-5259-a1ca-7f4b6d0a3c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(t+2)^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314122} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9058f6f-069f-5ad6-86e6-f5d49d5d96c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb569efa-7827-5c95-981f-32ffff356ddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-122345" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at $x=7\\,m$ and has velocity $v=12\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/634a0680d0f159006c524b8d0eb466b2c99b0f9b5a46cf38b7cd689c9d8d6fb2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6\\sqrt[3]{x+1}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=12\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=7\\,\\text{m}$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(12)^{2} & =\\frac{9(7+1)^{4/3}}{2}+K \\\\ 72 & =72+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{9(x+1)^{4/3}}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =9(x+1)^{4/3} \\\\ v & =\\pm 3(x+1)^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=3(x+1)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3(x+1)^{2/3}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3(x+1)^{2/3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3(x+1)^{2/3}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{x+1} & =t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=7$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=2,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x+1}=t+2.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{x+1} & =t+2 \\\\ x+1 & =(t+2)^{3} \\\\ x & =(t+2)^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8582586-2993-58ca-894d-f1b2ef69db0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.7\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314125} {:answer/id #uuid "f94514ab-0f3c-54c7-9a2c-d737fdd51916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314126} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc76b21-dc60-5a6e-92c7-eb9926209590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314127} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f35de8-12c0-5363-aa34-016ba8b66bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314128} {:answer/id #uuid "abff615b-88fa-5e83-979e-bf362569b025" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f94514ab-0f3c-54c7-9a2c-d737fdd51916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314126} :answer-field/id #uuid "18e0157c-d850-5e2a-a393-20dde143490c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "468eb29a-5370-5275-b5a4-4d18d8cf18e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-122347" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/3c5450b18c5dc58b082faadf4086b6a21c0c9b55afb016090147609a2ccfe719.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int [-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={3}^{1-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & ={3}^{1-2}+C \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-1}+\\frac{11}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{28}{3}} \\\\  & ≈-3.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8540e57d-b048-5ab3-aeaa-e7c48698f013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{2t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314132} {:answer/id #uuid "5f73179e-8a65-5462-9098-cbd299e605f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314133} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9d27b1-016c-540e-9806-3c428ea3a9f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+2)^{3}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314134} {:answer/id #uuid "c168ec6d-57b5-5f35-abb9-4c56118eb100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(12t-1)^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314135} {:answer/id #uuid "f391d876-9bc8-51c8-8822-3545affef68b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{t-8}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f73179e-8a65-5462-9098-cbd299e605f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314133} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba6e123a-1fab-58ba-a491-c61f45ab4aff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b41d621c-b89a-58ab-8c66-2280c7bf01d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122349" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=1\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14041958/assets/141c6dd6c91841238298b86fac1063c76faeb7cd103e13d2723f6ac05ed58f50.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(0+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x+2)^{3} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+2)^{3} & =12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=8,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{3}=12t+8.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3} & =12t+8 \\\\ x+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
